{
  "concept": "When two sides and a non-included angle (SSA) are given, comparing side a to the altitude h = b·sin(α) and to side b determines whether zero, one (right), two, or one triangle can be constructed.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "four side-by-side panels labeled (a), (b), (c), (d) with headings: 'No triangle, a < h', 'Right triangle, a = h', 'Two triangles, a > h, a < b', 'One triangle, a ≥ b'",
    "horizontal base line in each panel with angle α marked by an arc at the left end and vertex β labeled at the right end",
    "side b drawn as a fixed segment rising from α to apex γ, labeled 'b'",
    "apex labeled γ in each panel",
    "panel (a): short vertical segment labeled 'a' hanging from γ, stopping above the base with a visible gap",
    "panel (b): vertical side 'a' from γ reaching the base exactly at β, with a small right-angle square at β",
    "panel (c): solid vertical altitude from γ to the base labeled 'h', plus two dashed segments each labeled 'a' from γ to the base — one landing left of the altitude foot, one landing at β",
    "panel (d): side 'a' drawn as a solid slanted segment from γ down to β, completing a broad triangle",
    "interactive construction view: fixed α and b, candidate side a swinging from γ like a compass arc toward the base, with altitude h guide line"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "sideLengthA",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Side length a (α and b fixed)",
      "range": [0.5, 3, 0.01],
      "default": 0.8,
      "teaches": "The number of possible triangles is not arbitrary — it is decided entirely by comparing a to h and to b: below h nothing closes, at h exactly one right triangle, between h and b two triangles, at or above b exactly one"
    },
    {
      "id": "showAltitude",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show altitude h",
      "options": ["Hidden", "Shown"],
      "default": "Shown",
      "teaches": "The altitude h = b·sin(α) is the critical threshold: it is the shortest distance from γ to the base, which is why a < h can never reach the base and a = h reaches it at exactly one perpendicular point"
    },
    {
      "id": "caseStepper",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step through cases",
      "states": ["No triangle (a < h)", "Right triangle (a = h)", "Two triangles (h < a < b)", "One triangle (a ≥ b)"],
      "default": "No triangle (a < h)",
      "teaches": "Each inequality for a corresponds to one distinct geometric outcome — stepping through them in order shows the progression as a grows from 'too short to close' to 'long enough to swing past b'"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "No triangle: a < h",
      "narration": "Side a is shorter than the altitude h, the shortest possible distance from γ to the base — the segment dangles with a visible gap and no triangle can close.",
      "state": { "sideLengthA": 0.8, "showAltitude": "Shown", "caseStepper": "No triangle (a < h)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Right triangle: a = h",
      "narration": "When a exactly equals h, it just reaches the base at one perpendicular point — the right-angle marker appears and exactly one (right) triangle exists.",
      "state": { "sideLengthA": 1.5, "showAltitude": "Shown", "caseStepper": "Right triangle (a = h)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two triangles: h < a < b",
      "narration": "Now a is longer than h but shorter than b, so it can swing to the base on either side of the altitude foot — the two dashed positions both close, giving two valid triangles.",
      "state": { "sideLengthA": 2.1, "showAltitude": "Shown", "caseStepper": "Two triangles (h < a < b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "One triangle: a ≥ b",
      "narration": "Once a is at least as long as b, the left-hand landing point falls behind α and is no longer valid — only one triangle remains.",
      "state": { "sideLengthA": 2.7, "showAltitude": "Shown", "caseStepper": "One triangle (a ≥ b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Explore freely",
      "narration": "Drag the slider for a slowly and watch the construction pass through all four regimes; toggle the altitude off and on to confirm h is the boundary that separates 'impossible' from 'possible'.",
      "state": { "sideLengthA": 1.5, "showAltitude": "Shown", "caseStepper": "Right triangle (a = h)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The ambiguous case of SSA is fully resolved by two comparisons: a against h = b·sin(α) decides whether any triangle exists, and a against b decides whether one or two exist.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the four-panel static figure faithfully — identical labeling scheme across panels (α with arc at left of horizontal base, β at right, γ apex, b from α to γ, a from γ), panel headings exactly as in the original, panel (a) showing the dangling a with visible gap, panel (b) with the right-angle square at β, panel (c) with solid altitude h and two dashed a segments, panel (d) as a broad closed triangle. Alongside (or as the main interactive view), build a single live construction: fixed α (about 35–40°) and fixed b, with side a pivoting from γ whose length is set by the slider; draw a faint arc showing where the tip of a can land on the base. When altitude is shown, draw the dashed vertical h with right-angle marker at its foot. Highlight the active case's closing side(s) or gap as the stepper advances, and show the current inequality (e.g. 'h < a < b → two triangles') as a caption. Use one consistent color for fixed elements (black/dark) and an accent color (e.g. blue for a, red for h) so the changing quantity stands out."
}

